y in this farm's data.\n\n# FARM\nName: Demo Farm\nCrop: sugarcane\nLocation: Mandya\nFarmer: Demo Farmer\n\n# CURRENT OBSERVATION\n- Time: 2026-09-23 04:25 UTC (node demo-node-1)\n- Sensor values: as in SENSOR READINGS below\n- GPS: 12.806987279716143, 74.93193321230041\n\n# SENSOR READINGS (latest)\n- Taken: 2026-09-23 04:25 UTC\n- Temperature: 27.9 C\n- Humidity: 72.4 %\n- Soil moisture: 32.3 %\n\n# RECENT HISTORY\nOnly one observation on record — no comparison possible yet.\nObservations on record: 10 (most recent first).\n\n# ACTIVE ALERTS\nAddress these FIRST:\n- [critical] Node offline (no contact for 49530 min)\n\n# AGRICULTURAL KNOWLEDGE\nSugarcane Planting Methods and Advanced Nursery Techniques — Main Information:\n- **Tissue culture**: enables rapid multiplication of high-yielding, disease-resistant varieties; conventional vegetative propagation requires large seed quantities and has a low multiplication rate (10-12 years from variety release to field-scale coverage); tissue culture can compress this multiplication timeline to about 5 years and produces disease-free plantlets from apical meristem tissue.\n- **Spaced Transplanting (STP)**: raised nursery beds (3 ft wide) are prepared with FYM; single-bud setts are dipped in 0.2% Carbendazim, planted vertically, and treated with chlorpyriphos or imidacloprid against pests. Settlings are transplanted after 6–7 weeks, achieving 90–95% survival with careful management. Seed cane requirement drops to ~20 quintals/ha versus ~60 quintals/ha for normal planting.\n\nSugarcane Cultivation in India: Agro-Climatic Overview (with Karnataka Focus) — Important Recommendations:\n- Farmers should select varieties matched to their specific agro-climatic zone and planting season (early vs mid-late vs late) rather than using a single variety across all conditions, to balance cane tonnage with sugar recovery.\n- In Karnataka and other Adsali-planting tropical states, farmers with adequate water availability can consider Adsali (July–August) planting for higher yield and sugar recovery, provided irrigation water is not a limiting constraint.\n- Because sugarcane occupies land for 12–18 months, crop diversification through intercropping or sequential cropping (e.g., paddy–sugarcane–ratoon–finger millet/sesamum/blackgram systems, which are documented for Karnataka's peninsular zone) is recommended to generate mid-season income and reduce monoculture risk.\n\nSugarcane Cultivation in India: Agro-Climatic Overview (with Karnataka Focus) — Main Information:\n- Sugarcane requires a mean temperature of 28–32°C for optimal growth; temperatures above 45°C reduce tillering, and below 20°C slow growth. Areas with minimum temperature under 5°C are unsuitable.\n- The crop needs 75–120 cm of annual rainfall (if unirrigated) and a long growing season of 10–18 months. Ideal relative humidity is 70–85% during growth and 55–75% during ripening; below 50% humidity during growth is unsuitable.\n- India has two broad sugarcane regions:\n  - **Tropical region** (Maharashtra, Karnataka, Tamil Nadu, Andhra Pradesh, Gujarat, Madhya Pradesh, Kerala): ~45% of area, 55% of national production; productivity ~80 t/ha.\n\n# CONVERSATION MEMORY (recent turns, for context)\nFarmer: Prathi irrigation ge eshtu cm neeru hakbeku.\nGage (earlier, shortened): Please double-check before acting: \"There is not enough evidence from the latest observation\" (not in Gage's sources); \"Current crop growth stage (days after planting) is missing\" (not in Gage's sources); \"Recent irrigation history is ...\nFarmer: ಪ್ರತಿ irrigation ಗೆ ಎಷ್ಟು cm water ಹಾಕ್ಬೇಕು?\nGage (earlier, shortened): ದಯವಿಟ್ಟು ಮುಂದುವರಿಯುವ ಮೊದಲು ಪರಿಶೀಲಿಸಿ: \"If soil moisture <30% increase irrigation to 8 cm\" (Gage ಮಾಹಿತಿ ದಾಖಲೆಗಳಲ್ಲಿ ಇಲ್ಲ); \"If soil moisture >35% keep irrigation at 7 cm\" (Gage ಮಾಹಿತಿ ದಾಖಲೆಗಳಲ್ಲಿ ಇಲ್ಲ); \"Node repair involves checking power, ...\nFarmer: VKSA ಅಂತ ಒಂದು programme ಇದೆ ಅಂತ ಕೇಳಿದ್ದೀನಿ, ನಮ್ಮ ಕರ್ನಾಟಕದಲ್ಲಿ ಬಂತಾ?\nGage (earlier, shortened):