 Keep a simple log of irrigation dates, water depth applied, and any manual moisture readings to build a short‑term history.  \n\n- **When to seek expert help**  \n  1. If the node remains offline for more than 24 hours despite troubleshooting.  \n  2. If manual soil checks repeatedly show moisture below 30 % or the crop shows persistent wilting/yellowing after irrigation.  \n  3. If you need precise scheduling based on crop stage (DAP) and rainfall integration, contact the local agronomy extension office or a qualified irrigation specialist.", "claims": [], "unsupported": [], "final_answer": "**Observation**  \n- Active alert: **[critical] Node offline (no contact for 49 530 min)**  \n- Latest sensor reading (2026‑09‑23 04:25 UTC):  \n  - Temperature: 27.9 °C  \n  - Humidity: 72.4 %  \n  - Soil moisture: 32.3 %  \n\n**Analysis**  \n- The node has been offline for an extended period, so the soil‑moisture value cannot be confirmed as current; field conditions may have changed since the last transmission.  \n- The last recorded moisture (≈32 %) is just above the typical lower‑limit threshold (~30 %) for sugarcane, indicating the soil is marginally moist but close to the point where irrigation may be needed.  \n\n**Confidence** – **Medium** – because we have only a single, possibly outdated moisture reading and no information on crop age or recent rainfall.  \n\n**Recommendations**  \n\n- **Immediate actions**  \n  1. Verify the node’s power and communication (check battery, antenna, GSM/LoRa link) and restore connectivity.  \n  2. If the node cannot be fixed today, perform a quick manual check of soil moisture (e.g., feel the soil 15 cm deep or use a hand‑held meter).  \n  3. Apply **7–8 cm** of water per irrigation as a baseline for sugarcane, adjusting only if the manual check shows the soil is already adequately moist.  \n\n- **Monitoring advice**  \n  1. After irrigation, re‑measure soil moisture manually or wait for the node to come back online; aim for a reading