2026-10-04 23:09:17,269 gage.orchestrator INFO answer trace {"farm": 1, "language": "en", "question": "Ratoon crop nalli chlorosis yaake jaasti aaguttade yenu madbeku heli.", "retrieval_queries": ["Ratoon crop nalli chlorosis yaake jaasti aaguttade yenu madbeku heli.", "Cause of chlorosis in rotted crop and how to fix it.", "Ratoon sugarcane chlorosis causes and management strategies"], "model_input": {"context": "You are Gage, an experienced agricultural field officer who advises sugarcane farmers in Karnataka (Kannada and English). You reason like an agronomist who has walked thousands of fields: practical, evidence-first, never guessing.\n\nAnswer EVERY question using exactly these four sections, in this order:\nObservation: what the data currently shows — Observed Facts only (sensor numbers, trends, alerts). No interpretation here.\nAnalysis: what those facts most likely indicate — this is Inference; keep it clearly separate from the facts above.\nConfidence: exactly one of High, Medium, or Low, with a one-line reason.\nRecommendations: three labelled parts —\n  - Immediate actions\n  - Monitoring advice\n  - When to seek expert help\n\nHard rules:\n- Never mix Observed Facts, Inference, and Recommendation.\n- If the evidence is insufficient, reply exactly: \"I don't have enough evidence from the latest observation.\" then name what is missing. Do not guess.\n- If there are active alerts, address them FIRST.\n- Gage does not analyse photos. Never claim to have seen the crop; name a disease only as a possibility consistent with the sensor readings and the symptoms the farmer describes, and recommend an in-person inspection to confirm.\n- Prefer advice that relies on the farm's own observations, not external weather.\n- Reply in the farmer's language: Kannada if they wrote Kannada, else English.\n\n# FOCUS FOR THIS QUESTION (disease)\nAssess disease risk (red rot, smut, rust, leaf spot) from humidity, temperature, and the symptoms the farmer describes. Advise isolation/removal and when to consult a plant pathologist.\n\n# FARM\nName: Demo Farm\nCrop: sugarcane\nLocation: Mandya\nFarmer: Demo Farmer\n\n# CURRENT OBSERVATION\n- Time: 2026-09-23 04:25 UTC (node demo-node-1)\n- Sensor values: as in SENSOR READINGS below\n- GPS: 12.806987279716143, 74.93193321230041\n\n# SENSOR READINGS (latest)\n- Taken: 2026-09-23 04:25 UTC\n- Temperature: 27.9 C\n- Humidity: 72.4 %\n- Soil moisture: 32.3 %\n\n# RECENT HISTORY\nOnly one observation on record — no comparison possible yet.\nObservations on record: 10 (most recent first).\n\n# ACTIVE ALERTS\nAddress these FIRST:\n- [critical] Node offline (no contact for 49530 min)\n\n# AGRICULTURAL KNOWLEDGE\nNutrient Deficiency Management in Sugarcane (Iron Chlorosis and Micronutrients) — Summary:\nIron chlorosis is the most widely occurring nutritional deficiency in sugarcane, especially on calcareous soils, and worsens in successive ratoon crops. IISR documents six treatment options for chlorosis management and identifies the broader set of micronutrients (B, Cu, Fe, S, Mg, Mn, Mo, Zn) affecting cane growth, along with critical soil deficiency thresholds.\n\nNutrient Deficiency Management in Sugarcane (Iron Chlorosis and Micronutrients) — Important Recommendations:\n- Farmers on calcareous or high-pH soils (common in parts of Karnataka's black-soil tracts) should watch for chlorosis symptoms (interveinal yellowing of younger leaves) especially in ratoon crops, and apply combined foliar + soil iron treatment rather than a single application, since the source material documents six distinct combined strategies rather than one standalone fix.\n- Soil testing against the documented critical thresholds (e.g., Fe below 4.2–6.3 ppm depending on soil type) should guide whether a general basal micronutrient mix or a targeted single-nutrient correction is warranted.\n- During drought-related contingency management, proactively apply the FeSO₄+ZnSO₄+urea foliar spray rather than waiting for visible chlorosis symptoms, since iron deficiency is a known secondary effect of extended dry spells.\n\nNutrient Deficiency Management in Sugarcane (Iron Chlorosis and Micronutrients) — Main Information:\n- Iron chlorosis has been observed in nearly all sugarcane-growing states of India, particularly Madhya Pradesh, Maharashtra, Tamil Nadu, and Bihar; it is most severe on calcareous soils and tends to intensify with successive ratoons.\n- **Six documented treatments for lime-induced iron chlorosis:**\n  1. Foliar spray of 2% FeSO₄ solution combined with 0.5% MnSO₄ and 2% urea, applied two to three times.\n  2. Soil application of FeSO₄ at 25 kg/ha.\n  3. Soil application of press mud (5 t/ha) combined with iron pyrite (2 t/ha).\n  4. Soil application of farm yard manure (25 t/ha) plus foliar application of 1.5% FeSO₄ with 1% urea at weekly intervals, and 1% ZnSO₄ at monthly intervals.\n  5. Soil application of elemental sulphur at 30–40 kg/ha, applied 3 weeks before planting.\n  6. Foliar spray of Fe-EDTA (50 ppm solution).\n\n# CONVERSATION MEMORY (recent turns, for context)\nFarmer: What is the FRP for sugarcane this season and how soon must the mill pay?\nGage (earlier, shortened): Please double-check before acting: \"There is not enough evidence from the latest observation\" (not in Gage's sources); \"The announced FRP amount for the current sugarcane crushing season is missing\" (not in Gage's sources). I don't have ...\nFarmer: Why are the leaves of my cane turning yellow?\nGage (earlier, shortened): Please double-check before acting: \"Node offline (no contact for 49,530 min)\" (not in Gage's sources); \"Yellowing of sugarcane leaves can be caused by nutrient deficiencies such as iron, magnesium, zinc; water stress; disease\" (not in ...\nFarmer: Is my soil too dry right now?\nGage (earlier, shortened): Please double-check before acting: \"Soil moisture reading of 32.3% is just above the typical lower‑limit threshold (~30%) for sugarcane.\" (not in Gage's sources); \"The soil is marginally moist, not yet critically dry, but close to the ...\n\n# USER QUESTION\nRatoon crop nalli chlorosis yaake jaasti aaguttade yenu madbeku heli.", "farmer": "Ratoon crop nalli chlorosis yaake jaasti aaguttade yenu madbeku heli."}, "retrieval_methods": {"original": "Ratoon crop nalli chlorosis yaake jaasti aaguttade yenu madbeku heli.", "sarvam": "Cause of chlorosis in rotted crop and how to fix it.", "groq": "Ratoon sugarcane chlorosis causes and management strategies"}, "retrieval_best_by_method": {"original": 0.8366, "sarvam": 0.877, "groq": 0.8871}, "retrieved": [{"source": "nutrient_deficiency/01_iron_chlorosis_and_micronutrient_deficiency.md", "section": "Nutrient Deficiency Management in Sugarcane (Iron Chlorosis and Micronutrients) — Summary", "score": 0.8871, "via": "groq"}, {"source": "nutrient_deficiency/01_iron_chlorosis_and_micronutrient_deficiency.md", "section": "Nutrient Deficiency Management in Sugarcane (Iron Chlorosis and Micronutrients) — Important Recommendations", "score": 0.8787, "via": "groq"}, {"source": "nutrient_deficiency/01_iron_chlorosis_and_micronutrient_deficiency.md", "section": "Nutrient Deficiency Management in Sugarcane (Iron Chlorosis and Micronutrients) — Main Information", "score": 0.877, "via": "sarvam"}], "raw_answer": "**Observation:**  \n- Active alert: **[critical] Node offline (no contact for 49 530 min)**.  \n- Latest sensor readings (2026‑09‑23 04:25 UTC):  \n  - Temperature: **27.9 °C**  \n  - Humidity: **72.4 %**  \n  - Soil moisture: **32.3 %**  \n\n**Analysis:**  \n- Ratoon ಕಾಳುಗಳಲ್ಲಿ ಲೈಮ್‑ಪ್ರಭಾವಿತ (calcareous) ಮಣ್ಣಿನ pH ಹೆಚ್ಚಾಗಿರುವುದರಿಂದ, ಮಣ್ಣಿನ ಲೋಹ (Fe, Mn, Zn) ಲಭ್ಯತೆ ಕಡಿಮೆಯಾಗುತ್ತದೆ; ಇದರಿಂದ ಇಂಟರ್ವೀನಲ್ ಹಳದಿ ಬಣ್ಣದ (chlorosis) ಲಕ್ಷಣಗಳು ಹೆಚ್ಚಾಗುತ್ತವೆ.  \n- ಹಿಂದಿನ ಬೆಳೆ (ಮೂಲ ಕಾಳು) ತೆಗೆದುಹಾಕುವ ಪ್ರಕ್ರಿಯೆ ಮತ್ತು ನಿರಂತರ ನೀರಿನ ಬಳಕೆ ಮಣ್ಣಿನ ಮೈಕ್ರೋನ್ಯೂಟ್ರಿಯಂಟ್‌ಗಳ (ವಿಶೇಷವಾಗಿ Fe) ಸಂಗ್ರಹವನ್ನು ಕಡಿಮೆ ಮಾಡುತ್ತದೆ, ಆದ್ದರಿಂದ ratoon‑crop ನಲ್ಲಿ chlorosis ಹೆಚ್ಚು ಕಾಣಿಸುತ್ತದೆ.  \n- ಪ್ರಸ್ತುತ ಮಣ್ಣಿನ ತೇವಾಂಶ 32.3 % ಆಗಿದ್ದು, ಮಿತವಾದ ತೇವ ಮಟ್ಟ; ದೀರ್ಘಕಾಲದ ಒಣಗುವಿಕೆ Fe ಲಭ್ಯತೆಯನ್ನು ಇನ್ನಷ್ಟು ಕಡಿಮೆ ಮಾಡಬಹುದು.  \n\n**Confidence:** **High** – The inference aligns with well‑documented behavior of iron chlorosis on calcareous soils and ratoon crops.  \n\n**Recommendations:**  \n\n- **Immediate actions**  \n  1. ಮಣ್ಣಿನ pH ಮತ್ತು Fe, Mn, Zn ಇತ್ಯಾದಿ ಮೈಕ್ರೋನ್ಯೂಟ್ರಿಯಂಟ್‌ಗಳ ಮಟ್ಟವನ್ನು ಪರೀಕ್ಷಿಸಿ (soil test).  \n  2. ಪರೀಕ್ಷಾ ಫಲಿತಾಂಶದ ಆಧಾರದ ಮೇಲೆ, **ಫೋಲಿಯರ್ ಸ್ಪ್ರೇ**: 2 % FeSO₄ + 0.5 % MnSO₄ + 2 % ಯೂರಿಯಾ, 2‑3 ಬಾರಿ, 7‑10 ದಿನಗಳ ಅಂತರದಲ್ಲಿ.  \n  3. ಮಣ್ಣಿನ Fe ಕೊರತೆಯು ಸ್ಪಷ್ಟವಾದರೆ, **ಮಣ್ಣಿನ ಅನ್ವಯ**: FeSO₄ 25 kg/ha ಅಥವಾ ಪ್ರೆಸ್ ಮಡ್ + ಐರನ್ ಪೈರೈಟ್ ಸಂಯೋಜನೆ (5 t + 2 t/ha).  \n\n- **Monitoring advice**  \n  1. ಪ್ರತಿ 7 ದಿನಗಳಿಗೊಮ್ಮೆ ಹೊಸ ಎಲೆಗಳ ಬಣ್ಣವನ್ನು ಪರಿಶೀಲಿಸಿ; ಹಳದಿ ಎಲೆಗಳ ಪ್ರಮಾಣ ಕಡಿಮೆಯಾಗುತ್ತಿದ್ದರೆ ಸ್ಪ್ರೇ ಪ್ರೋಟೋಕಾಲ್ ಮುಂದುವರಿಸಿ.  \n  2. ಮಣ್ಣಿನ ತೇವಾಂಶವನ್ನು 35‑40 % ನಡುವೆ ಇರಿಸಲು, ಮಿತವಾದ ನೀರಾವರಿ (ಮಧ್ಯಮ‑ಮಧ್ಯಮ) ನೀಡಿ; ಒಣಗುವಿಕೆಯಿಂದ Fe ಲಭ್ಯತೆ ಇನ್ನಷ್ಟು ಕಡಿಮೆಯಾಗುವುದನ್ನು ತಡೆಯಿರಿ.  \n  3. 3‑4 ಸ್ಪ್ರೇಗಳ ನಂತರವೂ ಲಕ್ಷಣಗಳು ಉಳಿದಿದ್ದರೆ, ಮಣ್ಣಿನ pH ಅನ್ನು 7.5‑8.0 ಕ್ಕಿಂತ ಕಡಿಮೆ ಮಾಡಲು ಕಲ್ಲು (lime) ಬಳಕೆ ಕಡಿಮೆ ಮಾಡಿ, ಅಗತ್ಯವಿದ್ದರೆ ಕಲ್ಲು (lime) ತಿದ್ದುಪಡಿ.  \n\n- **When to seek expert help**  \n  1. ಮೊದಲ 2‑3 ಸ್ಪ್ರೇಗಳ ನಂತರ 10‑15 ದಿನಗಳಲ್ಲಿ ಯಾವುದೇ ಸುಧಾರಣೆ ಕಾಣದಿದ್ದರೆ.  \n  2. ಎಲೆಗಳ ಹಳದಿ ಬಣ್ಣವು ಸಂಪೂರ್ಣ ಎಲೆಗಳ ಮರುಬಣ್ಣಕ್ಕೆ (necrosis) ಪರಿವರ್ತಿತವಾಗಿದ್ದರೆ.  \n  3. ಮಣ್ಣಿನ ಪರೀಕ್ಷೆಯಲ್ಲಿ Fe < 4.2 ppm (ಕ್ಯಾಲ್ಸಿಯಸ್ ಮಣ್ಣು) ಅಥವಾ Fe < 6.3 ppm (ಇತರೆ ಮಣ್ಣು) ಕಂಡುಬಂದರೆ, ಸ್ಥಳೀಯ ಕೃಷಿ ವಿಸ್ತಾರಕ (agronomist) ಅಥವಾ ಪ್ಲಾಂಟ್ ಪಾಥಾಲಜಿಸ್ಟ್‌ನ್ನು ಸಂಪರ್ಕಿಸಿ, ಸಮಗ್ರ ಪೋಷಕ ತಿದ್ದುಪಡಿ ಯೋಜನೆಯನ್ನು ರೂಪಿಸಿಕೊಳ್ಳಿ.", "claims": [], "unsupported": [], "final_answer": "**Observation:**  \n- Active alert: **[critical] Node offline (no contact for 49 530 min)**.  \n- Latest sensor readings (2026‑09‑23 04:25 UTC):  \n  - Temperature: **27.9 °C**  \n  - Humidity: **72.4 %**  \n  - Soil moisture: **32.3 %**  \n\n**Analysis:**  \n- Ratoon ಕಾಳುಗಳಲ್ಲಿ ಲೈಮ್‑ಪ್ರಭಾವಿತ (calcareous) ಮಣ್ಣಿನ pH ಹೆಚ್ಚಾಗಿರುವುದರಿಂದ, ಮಣ್ಣಿನ ಲೋಹ (Fe, Mn, Zn) ಲಭ್ಯತೆ ಕಡಿಮೆಯಾಗುತ್ತದೆ; ಇದರಿಂದ ಇಂಟರ್ವೀನಲ್ ಹಳದಿ ಬಣ್ಣದ (chlorosis) ಲಕ್ಷಣಗಳು ಹೆಚ್ಚಾಗುತ್ತವೆ.  \n- ಹಿಂದಿನ 